# Installation instructions

How to install Sessions RAM Reclaimer on a Linux host, step by step. The result is a single `cron` entry that runs the reclaimer on a schedule and reaps idle Claude Code sessions. No secrets, no root access, no daemon — just a script, a cron line, and (optionally) two small system packages.

Takes about five minutes.

> Platform: Linux only. Relies on GNU `coreutils` (`stat -c %Y`) and the `/proc` filesystem. Does not work on macOS or BSD as-is.

> What else ships here: this install covers the reclaimer itself. The repo also ships an optional Claude Code skill (`/dontkillsession`) that lets you protect sessions from inside Claude for a duration you choose. See the [Companion skill](#companion-skill-optional) section at the end of this document.


## Placeholders used in this document

- `<user>` — the Linux user that owns the Claude Code sessions to be reaped (and therefore the user the cron entry runs as).
- `<install-dir>` — the absolute path where this `sessions-ram-reclaimer/` directory lives on disk.


## Prerequisites

The reclaimer is a plain `bash` script. The only runtime dependencies that may not be present on a minimal Linux install are the ones below.

The script also uses `python3` (for parsing the optional `do-not-kill-sessions.json` file). `python3` is pre-installed on Debian/Ubuntu by default; if yours is a stripped-down container image without it, add it to the check and install.

### What you need and why

| Command   | Debian/Ubuntu package | What the reclaimer uses it for |
| --------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `pstree`  | `psmisc`              | Walks the process tree inside a `tmux` pane to find the `claude` child, during the second-pass tmux reaping. |
| `tmux`    | `tmux`                | Required for the second pass to exist at all — reaping Claude sessions hosted inside `tmux` (for example, those launched by the `persistentagent` skill). |
| `python3` | `python3`             | Parses the `do-not-kill-sessions.json` file. Pre-installed on standard Debian/Ubuntu; may be missing on minimal container images. |

Everything else the script uses (`bash`, `ps`, `stat`, `date`, `find`, `printf`, `cron`) is already present on a standard Debian or Ubuntu system.

### Check what you already have

Paste this. It prints the name of anything missing, and nothing at all if your system has all three:

```
for cmd in pstree tmux python3; do
  command -v "$cmd" >/dev/null || echo "missing: $cmd"
done
```

### Install only what's missing

Map each missing name to its package and install only those:

| Missing       | Package to install |
| ------------- | ------------------ |
| `pstree`      | `psmisc`           |
| `tmux`        | `tmux`             |
| `python3`     | `python3`          |

E.g. if the check printed `missing: pstree` and `missing: tmux`, run `sudo apt install psmisc tmux`. If it printed nothing, skip this step.

### Access and permissions

- The reclaimer must run as the same Linux user that owns the Claude Code sessions. Detection reads `/proc/<pid>`; termination sends signals. Both need matching UID.
- No root required.
- No secrets file — the tool does not authenticate to anything. Everything it needs is local.
- The install directory must be writable by `<user>`, because the log file (`activity-log/sessions-ram-reclaimer.log`) lands there by default.


## Step 1: Place the files

Clone the repository into a stable path on disk, owned by `<user>`. That path is `<install-dir>` throughout the rest of this document.

```
git clone https://github.com/elias-coderunner/sessions-ram-reclaimer.git <install-dir>
```

Make the script executable:

```
chmod +x <install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

Confirm it runs on a fresh install:

```
<install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
tail -1 <install-dir>/activity-log/sessions-ram-reclaimer.log
```

On a fresh install with no idle sessions, you'll see a line like:

```
2026-11-24 19:03:53  verification executed, 0 idle sessions detected, 0 killed, 0 MB of RAM reclaimed
```

That is the expected baseline — the plumbing works; the first real idle session will be reaped on the next run.


## Step 2: (Optional) Customize

The reclaimer reads seven environment variables. All have sensible defaults, so this entire step is optional — skip it to run on defaults.

| Variable                | Default                                | What it does                                                                                           |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `IDLE_THRESHOLD`        | `21600` (6 hours, in seconds)          | How long a session must sit untouched before it is eligible for reaping.                               |
| `SKIP_SESSIONS`         | empty                                | Comma-separated Claude session UUIDs that must never be killed.                                        |
| `SKIP_TMUX`             | empty                                | Comma-separated `tmux` session names that must never be killed.                                        |
| `LOG`                   | `<install-dir>/activity-log/sessions-ram-reclaimer.log` | Where the per-run summary line is appended.                                                            |
| `CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR`   | `$HOME/.claude/projects`               | Where Claude Code keeps session transcripts. Override only if yours live somewhere else.               |
| `DO_NOT_KILL_FILE`      | `<install-dir>/do-not-kill-sessions.json` | JSON file of sessions to protect. Populated by the `/dontkillsession` skill, or edited by hand. Override to share one exclusion list across multiple installs. |
| `DRY_RUN`               | unset                                | Set to `1` for an ad-hoc preview run that reports what would be reaped without killing anything. Not for cron; use at the shell. |

Where to put overrides: either inline in the cron line (see Step 3), or in a small wrapper script next to the reclaimer that exports the variables and then `exec`s the script. Inline is simpler for one or two overrides; a wrapper is cleaner for several.


## Step 3: Install the cron entry

The reclaimer is a one-shot script. It is driven by `cron`, not `systemd` — there is no daemon to manage.

Open `<user>`'s crontab:

```
crontab -e
```

Add one line. The default is hourly, at minute 0:

```
0 * * * * <install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

To use a different frequency, change the cron expression. A few examples:

- Every 15 minutes: `*/15 * * * *`
- Every 6 hours: `0 */6 * * *`
- Once a day at 04:00: `0 4 * * *`

Pick a frequency that is small relative to `IDLE_THRESHOLD`. Hourly is a good match for the default 6-hour threshold; running more often than that is harmless but gives no additional benefit.

If you want to pass overrides from Step 2 inline, extend the line:

```
0 * * * * IDLE_THRESHOLD=43200 SKIP_TMUX=persistent-main <install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

The cron entry must belong to `<user>` — see the Access and permissions note above.


## Step 4: Verify

Three commands, in this order.

Dry-run — see what would be reaped right now without killing anything:

```
DRY_RUN=1 <install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

Real run — the same reaping the cron job will do at its next tick:

```
<install-dir>/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

Check the log:

```
tail -5 <install-dir>/activity-log/sessions-ram-reclaimer.log
```

You should see lines like:

```
2026-11-24 19:03:53  verification executed, 2 idle sessions detected, 2 killed, 531 MB of RAM reclaimed
```

Fields:

- verification executed — a real run (dry-runs print to the terminal only, nothing to log).
- N idle sessions detected — how many sessions crossed `IDLE_THRESHOLD`.
- N killed — how many were actually terminated.
- X MB of RAM reclaimed — total resident memory freed. Replaced by `RAM reclaimed not captured` in the rare case where sessions were killed but `/proc/<pid>/status` couldn't be read in time to measure their RSS.
- N failed (appears only if non-zero) — how many the kill signal didn't successfully terminate.

Additional log lines that may appear on runs that touch `do-not-kill-sessions.json`:

- `pruned N expired/malformed entry(ies) from <path>` — the reclaimer removed stale entries from the do-not-kill file. Informational, not an error.
- `do-not-kill-sessions file <path> could not be parsed, ignoring` — the file is unreadable or not valid JSON. The reclaimer continues using only the env-var protection lists. Fix the file or delete it.


## When something goes wrong

- Cron doesn't fire — confirm the cron daemon is running (`systemctl status cron` or `systemctl status crond`), and that `crontab -l` as `<user>` shows the entry you added.
- Script runs but reaps nothing, ever — likely `CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR` points at the wrong directory, or `IDLE_THRESHOLD` is too large for your usage. Run with `DRY_RUN=1` and inspect; add `set -x` temporarily if you need visibility.
- `N failed` stays non-zero every run — the kill signal isn't reaching the target process. Most common cause: cron is running the script as a user that does not own the Claude processes (so `kill` is permission-denied). Confirm with `ps -o user= -p <pid>` versus `whoami` inside the cron environment.
- First pass works, second pass (tmux) reaps nothing — `tmux` isn't installed, or the `tmux` server is running under a different user than the reclaimer. Re-run the Prerequisites check.
- First pass works, second pass reaps less than expected — `pstree` (`psmisc`) isn't installed, so the script falls back to the bare pane PID and may miss `claude` processes nested deeper in the pane's process tree. Install `psmisc`.
- Log grows unbounded — the reclaimer does not rotate its own log. Pipe it through `logrotate`, or set `LOG` to a destination that handles rotation on its own.


## Companion skill (optional)

This repository ships with a Claude Code skill named `dontkillsession` (folder: `./dontkillsession/`). The skill lets a user mark the Claude Code session they are currently in — or any set of sessions — as protected from reaping, for a duration they choose. It writes exclusions to `do-not-kill-sessions.json` in the reclaimer's install directory; the reclaimer reads the file on each run.

Installing the skill is not just a copy — it requires a one-time setup step that writes the install path and the user's timezone into `~/.config/sessions-ram-reclaimer/`. See the skill's own guide for the full procedure:

```
<install-dir>/dontkillsession/installation-instructions
```

The reclaimer works fine without the skill — the skill is a convenience layer, not a requirement. Protecting sessions without the skill is done via `SKIP_SESSIONS`, `SKIP_TMUX`, or by hand-editing `do-not-kill-sessions.json` in the install directory.


## Uninstall

Remove the cron line:

```
crontab -e    # delete the sessions-ram-reclaimer line
```

Remove the install directory:

```
rm -rf <install-dir>
```

If the skill was installed, remove it and its config:

```
rm -rf ~/.claude/skills/dontkillsession
rm -rf ~/.config/sessions-ram-reclaimer
```

Confirm no leftover processes (will be empty unless the reclaimer happens to be running right now — it is a short-lived cron script):

```
ps -ef | grep sessions-ram-reclaimer | grep -v grep
```

The reclaimer itself creates no files outside `<install-dir>`. The skill, if installed, adds files under `~/.claude/skills/dontkillsession/` and `~/.config/sessions-ram-reclaimer/`, which the commands above clean up.
